      "proposed_laureates": [
        "David Huang",
        "Eric A. Swanson",
        "James G. Fujimoto"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Optical coherence tomography made noninvasive, depth-resolved imaging of tissue practical and transformed ophthalmic diagnosis. This is a clear invention with broad benefit to medicine and a substantial optics contribution.",
        "attribution": "The proposed trio captures the closely connected clinical demonstration and enabling optical work. The boundary between the original concept, instrument development, and clinical translation deserves careful historical review.",
        "maturity": "Decades of widespread clinical use and continued technical development establish both the method's durability and its impact.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The relative priority and specific contributions of the early OCT teams need precise verification."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 2,
      "ballot_id": "B011",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Charles L. Bennett",
        "David N. Spergel",
        "Lyman A. Page Jr."
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "WMAP's precision mapping of the cosmic microwave background turned cosmological parameters into precision measurements and strongly established the standard cosmological picture.",
        "attribution": "The named leaders represent important instrument, analysis, and scientific contributions, but a major satellite mission depends on a much larger collaboration. Credit must identify original intellectual and experimental responsibility.",
        "maturity": "The results have been repeatedly tested against later observations, including those from Planck, and remain foundational.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "How to represent the contributions of the wider WMAP collaboration within the prize's laureate limit."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 3,
      "ballot_id": "B006",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Kam-Biu Luk",
        "Koichiro Nishikawa",
        "Yifang Wang"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Measuring a nonzero third neutrino mixing angle completed a central part of the neutrino mixing picture and enabled subsequent studies of CP violation. It is a consequential experimental discovery.",
        "attribution": "The credited leaders span the key experimental efforts. The committee should establish how the discovery was shared across experiments and distinguish leadership from the work of large collaborations.",
        "maturity": "The result has been confirmed by independent experiments and remains central to neutrino physics.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The priority and relative weight of the first measurements require detailed comparison of dates, data, and experimental contributions."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 4,
      "ballot_id": "B004",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Eva Y. Andrei",
        "Pablo Jarillo-Herrero",
        "Rafi Bistritzer"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Twisted bilayer graphene revealed that moiré engineering can produce flat bands and unexpected correlated phases, opening a new and experimentally fertile direction in quantum materials.",
        "attribution": "The experimental observations and theoretical prediction are both essential to the story. Allan MacDonald's contribution to the theoretical framework must be weighed carefully alongside Bistritzer's original prediction and the experimental teams.",
        "maturity": "The central phenomena have been reproduced and extended across devices and related materials, though their mechanisms and phase boundaries remain under study.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "Theoretical and experimental credit is distributed across several foundational contributions.",
          "The extent to which the field's defining results are settled remains an active question."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 5,
      "ballot_id": "B032",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Hidetoshi Katori",
        "Jun Ye"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Optical lattice clocks represent a major invention in precision measurement, enabling exceptional timekeeping and tests of fundamental physics with broad scientific reach.",
        "attribution": "Katori and Ye are central to the independent development of the method and its realization. The historical record should distinguish the core invention from subsequent clock improvements.",
        "maturity": "The method has been developed and independently validated over many years, with performance and applications still advancing.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The prize case depends on defining the invention's essential contribution against the broader history of optical clocks."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 6,
      "ballot_id": "B045",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Claudio Pellegrini",
        "Jörg Rossbach",
        "Kwang-Je Kim"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "High-gain X-ray free-electron lasers provide intense, ultrashort pulses that expose matter on atomic and molecular timescales, creating an unusually powerful new experimental tool.",
        "attribution": "The credited scientists represent important accelerator and FEL contributions, but the route from the key physical ideas to operating facilities involved many researchers and institutions.",
        "maturity": "Multiple operating facilities have demonstrated the method's capabilities and produced a sustained body of new science.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The original contributions and their relationship to earlier FEL and accelerator work need to be disentangled."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 7,
      "ballot_id": "B021",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Akira Ohtomo",
        "Harold Y. Hwang",
        "Jochen Mannhart"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "The discovery of conducting and correlated electronic phases at oxide interfaces showed that atomically engineered boundaries can host properties absent from the constituent bulk materials.",
        "attribution": "The proposed names are closely associated with the early experimental discovery and its development. Because interface growth, structural characterization, and interpretation were collaborative, individual roles need careful reconstruction.",
        "maturity": "The finding prompted extensive follow-up and remains important, although some early claims and the broader range of interface phenomena have required continuing experimental scrutiny.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The novelty and experimental reproducibility of particular early interface claims should be separated from the lasting general result."
        ]
      }
    },
